# Commit Often

Uncommitted work can be lost by a single bad command. Keep the working tree close to the last commit.

## When to commit

- At the end of every task, always.
- After every finished step of a multi-step task (a backend change, a migration, a frontend panel, a docs update).
- Before any risky operation: bulk find-and-replace, scripted edits, file moves, `git` commands that touch the working tree.

## How

PowerShell, `;` between commands:

```powershell
git add -A; git commit -q -m "Tsar appoints Governors: API endpoint and tests"
```

- Short message describing what the step did, not how.
- A step may be committed even if the build is not yet green; say "(work in progress)" in the message.
- Before `git add -A`, check `git status --short` for files that look unrelated or still being generated (for example sprite folders) and mention them to the user instead of committing them silently.
- Do not push unless the user asks.

## Never discard uncommitted work

```powershell
# ❌ BAD: wipes every uncommitted change in the file, not only your own
git checkout -- web/src/game/VillageWorld.ts

# ✅ GOOD: commit first, then undo your own mistake from the commit history
git add -A; git commit -q -m "Before fixing broken edit"
git diff HEAD~1 -- web/src/game/VillageWorld.ts
```

- Never run `git checkout -- <file>`, `git restore`, `git reset --hard`, `git clean` or `git stash drop` on files with uncommitted changes without asking the user first.
- If an edit went wrong, fix it with targeted edits, or ask the user to undo in the editor.
